<h2>Two statutes, two questions</h2>
<p>The criminal case asks whether the State has proved, beyond reasonable doubt, that the accused subjected the woman to cruelty as defined — conduct likely to drive her to suicide or cause grave injury, or harassment for dowry. The DV Act asks a different question: whether, on a preponderance of probabilities, the aggrieved person has experienced domestic violence as defined in Section 3 — a definition markedly wider, embracing physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and economic abuse — and if so, what protective and remedial orders the situation requires. A respondent can fail to be a criminal and still be a person against whom residence or monetary orders are warranted.</p>

