<h2>What kind of acquittal is it?</h2>
<p>Not all acquittals carry the same weight when cited in the DV proceeding. A judgment that examines the allegations and positively disbelieves them gives the respondent genuine material: he can argue that the very incidents pleaded in the DV application were tested and found wanting. An acquittal recorded because the complainant and her witnesses turned hostile, or because the parties compromised, or on a technical ground, decides much less — it establishes only that the prosecution&rsquo;s evidence fell short of the criminal standard on that day. Magistrates hearing DV applications routinely draw this distinction, and counsel should present the acquittal judgment itself, not merely its result.</p>

<h2>The respondent&rsquo;s toolkit after acquittal</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Place the judgment on record.</strong> File a certified copy in the DV proceeding with an application explaining its relevance to the overlapping allegations.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Section 25(2) application.</strong> Where interim monetary or residence orders were influenced by the pendency of the criminal case, seek alteration on the ground of changed circumstances.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Cross-examination.</strong> Use the criminal record — prior statements, contradictions established at trial — to confront the aggrieved person&rsquo;s evidence in the DV case.</div>
</div>

<h2>The aggrieved person&rsquo;s answer</h2>
<p>For the aggrieved person, the response is equally structured. First, emphasise the standard of proof: an acquittal is not a finding of innocence on the civil standard. Second, show the DV application&rsquo;s wider footprint — economic abuse, dispossession from the shared household, verbal and emotional abuse — much of which was never before the criminal court at all. Third, where the acquittal flowed from hostility or settlement pressure, say so candidly, since the circumstances of the acquittal are as relevant as its existence. The DV Act&rsquo;s machinery — including the Domestic Incident Report and the Protection Officer&rsquo;s material — remains available and unaffected.</p>

<h2>Practical sequencing for counsel</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Diarise both matters together; outcomes in one are events in the other, and the first lawyer to brief the court on them frames the narrative.</p>
  <p>In settlement discussions, price both tracks: a quashing or withdrawal on the criminal side without closure of DV reliefs — or vice versa — leaves the dispute half-alive.</p>
  <p>Remember Section 26 of the DV Act: reliefs under Sections 18 to 22 can also be claimed in other pending civil or criminal proceedings, so the architecture of parallel cases should be mapped before any strategic concession.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states the law in general terms for information only. The interplay of parallel proceedings is intensely fact-specific, and nothing here is advice in any particular matter.</div>
